Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
I.2.  Entering definitions of functions: light, rates, and F(t)\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]/FM, NPQ, and dO2/dt\
\>", "Subsection",
 CellFrame->{{0, 0}, {0.5, 0.5}},
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0.6, 0.4, 0.2],
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.931758233358055*^9, 3.9317582724531956`*^9}, 
   3.931770946158367*^9, 3.9317718408597984`*^9, 3.977209627443287*^9, {
   3.9773071056396904`*^9, 3.97730711626132*^9}, {3.977307375044073*^9, 
   3.9773073895016937`*^9}, {3.9773353612140255`*^9, 
   3.9773353870963116`*^9}, {3.9773355484194775`*^9, 3.977335584637455*^9}, {
   3.977335631841501*^9, 3.9773356691922207`*^9}, {3.9775465461926556`*^9, 
   3.977546549700615*^9}},
 FontSize->16,
 FontWeight->Bold,ExpressionUUID->"3cadea0d-b9e3-1f42-b771-1d5083fc781e"],

Cell["\<\
This section defines all time-dependent functions used by the model, \
including the light forcing L(t), kinetic rate expressions, and derived \
observables such as the normalized fluorescence yield F(t)/FM, \
non-photochemical quenching (NPQ), and the oxygen evolution rate dO2/dt.
In Mathematica, functions are defined symbolically using pattern arguments \
(for example t_) rather than explicit variable declarations. A definition of \
the form
f[t_] := expression means that f is evaluated only when a value for t is \
supplied. The operator := (SetDelayed) ensures that the right-hand side is \
recomputed each time the function is called, allowing parameters such as the \
forcing period T or state variables to vary between runs.
Many function definitions include guards of the form t_?NumericQ. These \
restrict evaluation to numerical arguments and are essential for stable \
numerical integration with NDSolve, preventing premature symbolic evaluation \
and improving solver robustness.
All functions defined in this section are intended for use inside numerical \
solvers and post-processing routines. They do not represent independent \
dynamical variables unless they are explicitly included in the system of \
differential or delay-differential equations.\
\>", "Text",
