 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0.6, 0.4, 0.2],
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9316167181482306`*^9, 3.931616725207141*^9}, 
   3.9316172604007163`*^9, {3.931617329225854*^9, 3.931617331570511*^9}, {
   3.931618560961228*^9, 3.931618561886388*^9}, {3.931758655571293*^9, 
   3.9317586654545097`*^9}, {3.9317587010901165`*^9, 3.931758757425419*^9}, {
   3.931759443826582*^9, 3.9317594539576263`*^9}, {3.931765976850231*^9, 
   3.931765981879921*^9}, 3.931766325094534*^9, {3.931771477482258*^9, 
   3.9317715012478504`*^9}, 3.9317718408597984`*^9, 3.9666257266214294`*^9, {
   3.966625762946089*^9, 3.9666257757482033`*^9}, {3.9666269362883205`*^9, 
   3.9666269365353165`*^9}, {3.9772096545071354`*^9, 
   3.9772096711677074`*^9}, {3.977335464650997*^9, 3.977335471076271*^9}, {
   3.97754633709873*^9, 3.977546403612154*^9}, {3.9775468481231575`*^9, 
   3.9775468584734535`*^9}, {3.9775468989944134`*^9, 
   3.9775469230248737`*^9}, {3.977550373798399*^9, 3.977550377315895*^9}, {
   3.977557181056284*^9, 3.977557184740883*^9}, 3.977557447352001*^9, {
   3.9777472732598495`*^9, 3.977747284697277*^9}},
 FontSize->16,
 FontWeight->Bold,ExpressionUUID->"55024cdb-f789-024a-a9c0-e2c880f195b4"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["In this section, we simulate the nonlinear \
photosynthetic electron-transport dynamics under harmonically modulated \
light. The system of ordinary differential equations defined in Section I is \
integrated in time while the incident light intensity varies periodically \
with period T and mean intensity constL.\nTo isolate the intrinsic dynamical \
response of the system to periodic forcing, simulations are initialized \
either from a well-defined dark reference state or from the steady state \
corresponding to constant illumination at constL (Section II). The latter \
provides a reproducible and physiologically consistent starting point for \
oscillatory forcing.\nFor each forcing period T, the model is integrated over \
multiple light cycles using a period-by-period (Poincar\[EAcute]) integration \
strategy. This approach enables detection of convergence to a periodic \
attractor (limit cycle) by comparing system states or selected observables at \
equivalent phases of successive forcing periods. Convergence is assessed \
using a user-selectable metric (convMetric), based either on the full state \
vector or on chlorophyll fluorescence.\nOnce convergence is detected, only \
the final NLastPeriods cycles are retained for analysis and export. Derived \
observables, including normalized chlorophyll fluorescence F/Fm\[Prime], \
non-photochemical quenching (NPQ), and the oxygen evolution rate, are \
evaluated algebraically from the converged state trajectories.\nBy repeating \
this procedure over a list of forcing periods (Tlist), we systematically \
characterize the frequency-dependent response of the photosynthetic system \
while keeping the underlying biochemical model, numerical solver, and \
convergence criteria unchanged.\n\nSchematic overview of the numerical \
simulation pipeline for periodically forced photosynthetic dynamics. For each \
forcing period \|01d447, the simulation proceeds through three conceptually \
distinct stages. First, numerical solver and convergence parameters (maximum \
number of periods, convergence window, relative and absolute tolerances, and \
integration method) are specified globally (Section III.1). Second, a \
period-chunked Poincar\[EAcute] integration algorithm is defined (Section \
III.2), which advances the system one forcing period at a time and detects \
convergence to a periodic attractor by comparing system states or selected \
observables at a fixed phase of successive cycles. This algorithm assumes \
that the governing equations already contain the appropriate time-periodic \
forcing and does not construct the equations itself.\n\nThird, for each \
specific forcing period \|01d447 (Section III.3), the governing ordinary \
differential equations are instantiated by embedding the periodic light \
modulation with period \|01d447 into the model equations. These equations are \
then passed to the Poincar\[EAcute] integration routine together with the \
chosen initial conditions. Convergence is declared when successive Poincar\
\[EAcute] samples agree within prescribed absolute and relative tolerances \
for a specified number of consecutive periods. Upon convergence, the periodic \
steady state and associated diagnostics are returned for further analysis. \
Correct execution requires that the same numeric value of \|01d447 be used \
consistently both in constructing the equations and in the period-chunked \
integration.",
 FontFamily->"Consolas",
 FontSize->12,
 FontWeight->"Normal"]], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9775498904294376`*^9, 3.9775499108171005`*^9}, {
