Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["V. Robustness of frequency-response estimation  to \
measurement noise and sampling",
 FontSize->16,
 FontWeight->"Bold",
 FontColor->RGBColor[0.6, 0.4, 0.2]]], "Title",
 CellFrame->{{0, 0}, {0.5, 0.5}},
 CellGroupingRules->{"TitleGrouping", Inherited},
 CellFrameColor->GrayLevel[0],
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9316978235922947`*^9, 3.9316978308224087`*^9}, {
   3.931754486545412*^9, 3.931754545732315*^9}, {3.9317581231118126`*^9, 
   3.931758125783575*^9}, {3.9317700222803783`*^9, 3.9317700263323917`*^9}, {
   3.9317704368845177`*^9, 3.9317704385617523`*^9}, {3.931770772415556*^9, 
   3.931770785692173*^9}, {3.931771054180374*^9, 3.9317710553727207`*^9}, {
   3.9317729424542007`*^9, 3.9317729802170506`*^9}, {3.9317730747321606`*^9, 
   3.9317730873414536`*^9}, {3.9317745242192774`*^9, 3.931774537994746*^9}, {
   3.9317746085717363`*^9, 3.931774614810775*^9}, {3.9317749409913845`*^9, 
   3.931774944049199*^9}, {3.931775166325892*^9, 3.931775200159012*^9}, 
   3.966625946278944*^9, 3.976858417488886*^9, 3.9772281563899536`*^9, 
   3.977549754527199*^9, {4.001396442660902*^9, 4.0013964608634663`*^9}},
 FontSize->18,
 FontWeight->Bold,
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.94, 0.91, 0.88],ExpressionUUID->"92c8899b-83b5-094c-afa6-17bf2c2537fc"],

Cell["\<\
### Objective and rationale
An important advantage of frequency-domain measurements is that the \
excitation waveform is precisely known. After convergence to a periodic \
steady state, the response is composed of harmonics of the excitation \
frequency. Rather than reconstructing an arbitrary time-dependent \
fluorescence signal, the analysis can therefore concentrate on estimating the \
amplitudes and phases of a limited number of harmonic components.
This use of prior information provides a statistical advantage when \
measurements are noisy. Synchronous estimation at the known excitation \
frequency suppresses contributions from uncorrelated noise, and the \
uncertainty of the estimated harmonic coefficients decreases approximately \
with the inverse square root of the number of independent observations.
The objective of this section is to quantify this advantage using synthetic \
fluorescence measurements generated from the mechanistic photosynthesis \
model. Specifically, we investigate how accurately the fundamental-harmonic \
gain and phase can be recovered when the fluorescence observations are \
affected by additive measurement noise and when the sampling density is varied.

### Numerical experiment
We use previously calculated, noise-free periodic fluorescence responses \
obtained at a mean illumination of 500 \[Micro]mol photons m\:207b\.b2 \
s\:207b\.b9 and a modulation amplitude of 25 \[Micro]mol photons m\:207b\.b2 \
s\:207b\.b9, for forcing periods of 1, 10, 100, and 1000 s.
For each forcing period, the final two cycles of the converged response \
provide the reference waveform. These waveforms are resampled at 128, 512, \
and 2048 observations per period. Independent, zero-mean Gaussian noise is \
then added to the sampled fluorescence values, with noise standard deviations \
corresponding to 0%, 25%, 50%, and 100% of the fundamental fluorescence \
amplitude.
For each combination of forcing period, sampling density, and noise level, \
100 independent noise realizations are generated. The fundamental Fourier \
coefficient of each noisy fluorescence record is calculated using the same \
Fourier convention as in the preceding model analysis. The illumination \
waveform is assumed to be known without measurement error.
The recovered complex frequency response is compared with the corresponding \
noise-free reference at the same sampling density. Estimation accuracy is \
characterized by the relative error in gain and the phase error in degrees. \
Their root-mean-square errors across independent realizations quantify the \
combined effects of random variability and estimation bias.

### Sampling density and experimental duration
Increasing the number of observations can improve the precision of harmonic \
estimation, but two strategies must be distinguished. Recording additional \
forcing periods increases the experimental duration, which can become \
prohibitive when investigating slow photosynthetic regulation. In contrast, \
increasing the sampling density does not extend the measurement duration, \
provided that the acquisition system supports the higher sampling rate.
The present experiment therefore keeps the observation window fixed at two \
forcing periods and varies only the number of observations per period. Under \
the assumption of independent measurement noise with constant variance per \
observation, increasing the sampling density is expected to improve \
estimation precision. This advantage may be reduced when noise is temporally \
correlated or when shorter acquisition intervals decrease the number of \
detected photons per observation.

### Scope and limitations
The analysis tests the numerical recovery of frequency-response \
characteristics under explicitly defined measurement conditions. It does not \
simulate physiological drift, correlated noise, illumination instability, or \
other nonstationary disturbances that may affect real experiments.
Furthermore, accurate recovery of gain and phase should not be confused with \
unique identification of the underlying biochemical processes or kinetic \
parameters. The latter requires additional experimental constraints and an \
explicit identifiability analysis.
The results are intended to demonstrate the statistical advantage of \
exploiting known harmonic excitation and to provide a quantitative basis for \
selecting sampling conditions in future experimental applications.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0013965699709015`*^9, 
  4.0013966009619617`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d47ba5fb-ce25-9b4e-a2d8-\
e34a2b226b7b"],

